steps.instructions:
Research only the angle in your item, from evidence: the code (file:line), its dependencies and docs, and measurements you can take without changing anything (timings, counts, sizes, versions, a dry run). Edit nothing. Use the web research below for anything outside the repository, and cite its URLs; when your angle needs more from outside, read the web yourself (WebSearch and WebFetch when you have them, else a read-only `curl -sL <url>`) and name each URL you used. Give numbers wherever you can and say how you got each one. Write your findings as a list of claims, each with its file:line or URL; trace the paths your angle touches (inbound, internal messages and their contract, persistence, outbound) and name the tests that exist for them; mark Unverified what no source confirms, with what must be confirmed. Name the risks and what the angle would cost to build (files, migration, deploy order). End with VERDICT: promising | possible | no.
What the web research found:
{{data.web_docs_text}}

Write the spike's findings for the person who decides, and for the feature spec that may start from them (the researchers' answers follow yours word for word in the report and the feature gets both, so carry their evidence, never summarise it away). In this order: the question and the decision; Findings (evidence), a list where every claim has its file:line or URL, with the paths the change touches traced (inbound, internal messages and their contract, e.g. a schema with additionalProperties: false, and the old messages already queued at deploy, persistence, outbound); the tests that exist today for those paths (or none); gaps that need a product decision, called decisions, not facts (with the framing's open questions for the spec: how to build it is the spec interview's to ask, not this spike's); Unverified, each fact no source confirms with what must be confirmed and by whom; a comparison table (angle, verdict, the key numbers, risk, effort); for the recommended angle, draft acceptance criteria "AC-n [API|WEB|E2E|SMOKE] Given, when, then" that are testable and cover absent, valid, malformed, a later update, an old queued message and the docs; Test layers per draft criterion (unit, functional, integration, smoke, e2e, or why not); Risks and cost (files touched, migration or not, the rolling deploy order between producer and consumer, ambiguities to decide); the sources with their URLs; open questions; and one recommendation with its reason. RECOMMEND is that recommendation on one line.
